course: dictionary
lesson: 3193
title: Endless and going on for ever
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Endless means without an end, going on for ever. «Endless sea» paints a picture of water that seems to reach the edge of the sky with no shore in sight.
explain: In everyday talk, endless often means seeming to go on for a very long time, or very many. «The list is endless» does not mean the list has no last line. It means there are far too many items to finish.
explain: Use the words around endless to check which meaning fits. «Replaying a failure can help you learn at first, but endless replays at night just keep the hurt fresh.» The replays are not forever. They are too many to stop, so swap in too many and see if it still makes sense.
fact: Endless means without an end, going on for ever.
fact: In everyday talk, endless can mean seeming to go on for a very long time, or very many.
fact: Endless is an adjective, so it describes a noun: «endless fun», «an endless supply».
fact: «Endless feeds» on apps keep you scrolling, which is why timers and app limits help.
fact: Films show the Sahara as endless golden dunes, but only about a quarter of it is sand; most of it is bare rock and gravel plains.
exercise: «Infinity Mirror Rooms make the space look endless.» What does endless mean here? | Very small and dark | Full of bright paint | *Seeming to have no end | Closed off with a locked door
exercise: «The list is endless.» In everyday talk, what does this mean? | Nobody has read the list | There are exactly ten items on the list | *There are far too many items to finish | The list was written in pencil
exercise: Apps use autoplay and «endless feeds». Find and copy the word that describes the feeds. | apps | *endless | keep | autoplay
test: «Replaying a failure can help you learn at first, but endless replays at night just keep the hurt fresh.» What does endless replays mean? | Replays on a bright sunny day | *Replays that keep going, too many to stop | Replays that stop after one try | Replays with no sound
test: In maths, the number line is truly endless. Which statement is true? | The number line stops at 100 | Numbers only go up to ten | Counting ends at the biggest number you know | *You can always count one more number
exam: Ruby says the desert is endless. What does she mean? | It is full of water | *It seems to go on without an end | It has a fence all around it | It is very small
